% You are an expert Python engineer. Implement the internal shipping domain types.

<include>context/project_preamble.prompt</include>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"types":[
  {"name":"Parcel"},
  {"name":"Address"},
  {"name":"Quote"},
  {"name":"NoServiceAvailable"},
  {"name":"ProviderTimeout"}]}}
</pdd-interface>

<responsibility>
Define the internal domain types shared across every shipping-rate integration:
Parcel, Address, Quote, and the exceptions NoServiceAvailable and ProviderTimeout.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
- Does not perform any network calls.
- Does not know about any specific carrier's wire format.
- Does not choose between carriers or persist quotes.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
- Quote: the result of a rate lookup. Carries an integer minor-unit value and a
  currency string. A reader must not be able to tell which carrier produced it.
</vocabulary>

<contract_rules>
T1 (MUST): Parcel carries the physical attributes needed to request a rate, in
our own metric units and no other: weight_grams (int), length_mm (int),
width_mm (int), height_mm (int). All must be positive.
T2 (MUST): Address carries the destination attributes needed to request a rate:
line1 (str | None), line2 (str | None), city (str), state_or_province
(str | None), postal_code (str), country_code (str, ISO 3166-1 alpha-2).
line1 and line2 are the customer's street address and are sensitive.
T3 (MUST): Quote carries exactly two fields: amount_minor_units (int) and
currency (str, ISO 4217).
T4 (MUST): NoServiceAvailable and ProviderTimeout are exceptions defined in this
same module.
T5 (MUST NOT): None of these types or exceptions reference any vendor, carrier,
or wire-format concept.
</contract_rules>

<coverage>
T3: story__price_in_minor_units.md
</coverage>
